# Working in {{PROJECT_NAME}}

Rules for any agent, human or otherwise. Each says what and why, because a rule
you understand adapts to a case it never anticipated and a rule you merely obey
does not.

## Read before writing

`docs/architecture.md` is the shape of this project. `docs/decisions.md` is why
it is that shape. Reading both takes two minutes and prevents the class of
change that works, passes review, and quietly contradicts a decision someone
made deliberately six months ago.

## Prefer the current documentation over your memory

Your training data is older than this project's dependencies. A plausible API
that no longer exists is the most expensive kind of wrong, because it survives
review by looking exactly like code that would work.

Check the version in the lockfile. Read the real signature. Cite where you read
it.

## Stay inside the architecture

If your change fits the architecture, make it. If it does not, the architecture
changes first, in `docs/decisions.md`, with the reasoning. Code that silently
departs from the documented shape is how a codebase stops matching its own map.

## Repair the defect, not the feature

Deleting behaviour to clear an error reaches zero errors without fixing
anything, and it passes every check that only looks for errors. Make the
smallest change that removes the cause. If a real fix requires removing a
feature, that is a decision for a person.

## Finish means finished

`docs/done.md` defines it: zero outstanding issues locally, in the tests, and
deployed. A check you could not run is recorded as outstanding, never skipped
quietly.

## Say less

Report the finding, not the search for it. No em dashes. No filler adjectives.
`docs/style.md` has the rest.

